WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:05.680
Treaty Feasibility and Cost Analysis Previous treaties failed because they ran on guilt.

2
00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:14.860
Guilt has terrible fuel efficiency. This one runs on greed, which is (regrettably) humanity's only reliable fuel source.

3
00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:20.140
"Getting one hundred and ninety-five countries to agree costs billions and takes forever."

4
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:28.340
No. Getting countries to implement things costs billions. Getting them to sign papers is surprisingly cheap.

5
00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:35.660
Your entire diplomatic system runs on the principle that writing your name on a document feels like accomplishing something

6
00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:41.680
(it doesn't, but it's affordable). What Treaties Actually Cost.

7
00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:48.300
N.G.O. campaign budgets are rarely disclosed publicly, but treaty campaigns run on pocket change compared to the thing they

8
00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:52.580
create. The Ottawa Treaty banning landmines took fourteen months.

9
00:00:53.320 --> 00:01:00.700
One staff member, Jody Williams, grew a coalition to over a thousand organizations across sixty countries.

10
00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:04.519
One hundred and twenty-two states signed. She won the Nobel Peace Prize.

11
00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:13.400
One woman with a phone convinced one hundred and twenty-two countries to stop burying explosives in the ground where children

12
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:19.900
walk. Your species is capable of remarkable things when one person gets sufficiently annoyed.

13
00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:28.280
The Cluster Munitions Convention took about two years. Seven governments and a coalition of N.G.O.s got ninety-four states

14
00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:34.040
to sign. The Arms Trade Treaty took a decade but started with three supporting governments.

15
00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:40.600
The Control Arms coalition published more than fifty reports until one hundred and thirty countries signed.

16
00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:49.200
The Nuclear Ban Treaty also took a decade. ICAN ran the entire operation with five staff in a Geneva office.

17
00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:53.980
One hundred and twenty-two states voted yes. Another Nobel Prize.

18
00:01:54.800 --> 00:02:02.030
Five people in a Geneva office won a Nobel Prize. You have more people in your accounting department.

19
00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:07.520
Pattern: Total to get a treaty signed: fifteen to fifty million dollars.

20
00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:14.350
Your species spends more than that on Super Bowl ads for beer (the thing that makes you temporarily stupider).

21
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:21.900
You will pay more to sell stupidity beverages during one sporting event than it costs to get one hundred and twenty-two

22
00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:27.580
countries to agree on anything. Inflation-Adjusted to twenty twenty-four Dollars.

23
00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:36.120
Using B.L.S. C.P.I. data: The Ottawa Treaty in nineteen ninety-seven cost between fifteen and twenty-five million dollars,

24
00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:43.240
which is thirty to fifty million in today's money. The Cluster Munitions Convention in two thousand and eight cost fifteen

25
00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:47.650
to twenty-five million, or twenty-three to thirty-eight million in twenty twenty-four dollars.

26
00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:55.160
The Arms Trade Treaty in twenty thirteen cost twenty to thirty million, which is twenty-seven to forty-one million today.

27
00:02:55.880 --> 00:03:03.480
The Nuclear Ban Treaty in twenty seventeen cost ten to twenty million, or thirteen to twenty-five million in twenty twenty-four

28
00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:11.140
dollars. In today's money: Treaty campaigns cost roughly twenty-five to fifty million dollars (twenty twenty-four U.S.D.).

29
00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:19.460
Banning landmines: roughly forty million dollars to get the signatures, and over five billion dollars to actually dig them

30
00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:26.620
up. The paperwork is always the cheap part. The expensive part is doing the thing you wrote on the paperwork (your species

31
00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:31.420
often skips this part). Cost for a one percent Health Treaty.

32
00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:38.380
Historical treaty campaigns ran on pocket change. But this is not just a signature campaign.

33
00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:48.620
It is a referendum engine, lobbying operation, diplomatic push, and reserve fund aimed directly at military budgets, which

34
00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:54.360
means you are picking a fight with the most powerful industry your species has ever created.

35
00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:59.100
The relevant planning number is not what it costs to collect signatures.

36
00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:04.560
It is what it costs to win a fight with the people who build aircraft carriers.

37
00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:14.460
Historical treaty-signing benchmark: N.G.O.-led campaigns to get signatures on paper cost twenty-five to fifty million dollars

38
00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:24.040
in twenty twenty-four U.S. dollars. A modeled one percent Treaty campaign covering referendum, lobbying, diplomacy, legal

39
00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:33.620
work, and a reserve fund costs one billion dollars. The modeled annual burn is an average spend across the campaign window

40
00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:37.940
of two hundred and fifty million dollars per year. The Math.

41
00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:48.660
Modeled campaign cost: one billion dollars. What the treaty unlocks: twenty-seven point two billion dollars per year.

42
00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:57.980
Forever. Even before you count downstream health gains, this is a one-year redirection of government spending far larger

43
00:04:57.980 --> 00:05:01.770
than the campaign that triggered it. You are not buying signatures.

44
00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:06.540
You are buying leverage. How to Make Governments Sign Things.

45
00:05:07.660 --> 00:05:09.260
Politicians move when they see voters demanding it and political cover to do it.

46
00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:14.320
Give them both. First, prove public demand. Get three point five percent of the population (two hundred and eighty million

47
00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:23.420
people) to say "yes" with verified identity. The three point five percent finding is not a magic law of treaty ratification.

48
00:05:23.980 --> 00:05:32.300
It is a coordination heuristic: the point where a cause stops looking like a niche moral hobby and starts looking like a

49
00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:37.030
voting bloc large enough to create career risk. That's not activism.

50
00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:47.300
That's a mandate. Second, prove it's safe. Peer-reviewed modeling showing every nation gains more in health savings than

51
00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:53.200
it loses in military spending. Politicians need cover the way vampires need darkness.

52
00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:59.880
Give them the numbers and they'll pretend it was their idea (the second most reliable pattern in your political history,

53
00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:06.740
after blaming the previous administration). Timeline to "Governments Can't Ignore This".

54
00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:14.740
With the first phase of a one billion dollar campaign, you can reach "Treaty-Ready" status in two to three years: a platform

55
00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:21.660
with two hundred and eighty million verified supporters (the three point five percent threshold), country-by-country impact

56
00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:29.560
modeling, and a coalition of one hundred and twenty plus supportive N.G.O.s and five to ten champion governments.

57
00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:35.620
Once these assets exist, formal negotiations become very hard to avoid.

58
00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:43.420
Not because politicians want them. Because ignoring two hundred and eighty million people is the kind of career decision

59
00:06:43.420 --> 00:06:49.510
that leads to "spending more time with family.". The slow part is not physical implementation.

60
00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:58.260
It is coalition activation. Once a political threshold is crossed, budget priorities can move much faster than peacetime

61
00:06:58.260 --> 00:07:06.980
common sense suggests. The United States cut military spending by eighty-seven point six percent in the two years after

62
00:07:06.980 --> 00:07:11.950
World War Two. The bottleneck is not whether states can reallocate fast.

63
00:07:12.380 --> 00:07:19.080
It is whether enough pressure, cover, and institutional machinery exist to make them do it.

64
00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:25.390
You aren't buying a treaty. You're buying the machinery that makes the treaty very hard to refuse.

65
00:07:26.680 --> 00:07:35.000
Political Success Probability: Economic Analysis. This section documents the rationale for the one percent central estimate

66
00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:41.580
(and a twenty-five percent optimistic scenario ceiling). The model assumes failure is the base case.

67
00:07:42.260 --> 00:07:47.680
It's still a good bet. On Wishonia, we would not invest in something where failure is the base case.

68
00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:53.200
On Earth, this is called "venture capital" and your richest humans do it professionally.

69
00:07:54.400 --> 00:08:02.200
Even at one percent probability of success, this intervention's expected cost per disability-adjusted life years, or D.A.L.Y.s

70
00:08:04.070 --> 00:08:12.860
($0.177) is still five hundred and three times more cost-effective than malaria bed nets ($89 per D.A.L.Y.).

71
00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:17.440
Bed nets are the gold standard of global health interventions.

72
00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:21.740
This beats the gold standard while assuming it will almost certainly fail.

73
00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:30.140
The math doesn't care about your pessimism. The political success probability point estimate is one percent, with a ninety-five

74
00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:34.000
percent confidence interval of zero point one percent to ten percent.

75
00:08:35.080 --> 00:08:42.100
The expected cost per D.A.L.Y. point estimate is zero point one seven seven dollars, with a ninety-five percent confidence

76
00:08:42.100 --> 00:08:47.200
interval of zero point zero two nine dollars to three dollars and twenty cents.

77
00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:54.860
The cost-effectiveness versus bed nets point estimate is five hundred and three times, with a ninety-five percent confidence

78
00:08:54.860 --> 00:09:00.900
interval of thirty times to three thousand times. The bed net cost per D.A.L.Y.

79
00:09:01.160 --> 00:09:07.560
point estimate is eighty-nine dollars, with a ninety-five percent confidence interval of seventy-eight dollars to one hundred

80
00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:17.060
dollars. Historical Precedents (The Depressing Table). For the zero point seven percent O.D.A., or Official Development

81
00:09:17.060 --> 00:09:25.580
Assistance, target of zero point seven percent of gross national income, or G.N.I., to foreign aid, only about twenty percent

82
00:09:25.580 --> 00:09:31.280
of countries complied over fifty plus years, which was basically just Scandinavia and Germany.

83
00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:38.660
The Kyoto Protocol set binding emissions targets that about fifty-five percent of countries initially followed before a

84
00:09:38.660 --> 00:09:43.200
collapse between nineteen ninety-seven and twenty twelve, noting the U.S.

85
00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:51.160
never ratified and Canada withdrew. The Paris Agreement for non-binding pledges has about fifteen to twenty-five percent

86
00:09:51.160 --> 00:09:58.220
of countries on track since twenty fifteen, where one hundred and ninety-five countries signed but maybe forty meant it.

87
00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:07.040
The NATO two percent G.D.P. defense spending target is met by about seventy-two percent of members as of twenty twenty-four,

88
00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:14.820
whereas only thirty-two percent complied from twenty fourteen until an actual land war in Europe motivated spending.

89
00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:24.480
The E.U. Stability Pact of a three percent deficit and sixty percent debt has been routinely violated since nineteen ninety-seven,

90
00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:32.820
with France and Germany breaking their own rule. Pattern: International financial commitments achieve less than twenty-five

91
00:10:32.820 --> 00:10:39.080
percent meaningful compliance. Your species signs things and then doesn't do them.

92
00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:46.760
It's like a New Year's resolution, but for geopolitics. The gym membership analogy is overused but accurate: purchased with

93
00:10:46.760 --> 00:10:53.260
enthusiasm in January, abandoned by February, cited in arguments about personal growth throughout March.

94
00:10:53.660 --> 00:11:00.240
Why a one percent Treaty is Harder Than Most Precedents. Let's be honest about the obstacles.

95
00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:03.900
This is not a chapter written by someone trying to sell you something.

96
00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:11.400
(It is. But at least it's honest about the hard parts.) It touches military budgets, and politicians would rather discuss

97
00:11:11.400 --> 00:11:19.940
their browser history than their defense budget. It requires ongoing annual allocation, not a one-time signature, which

98
00:11:19.940 --> 00:11:24.740
makes it a gym membership for peace (monthly, forever, no quitting).

99
00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:33.160
There's no immediate security threat driving it; one hundred and fifty thousand deaths per day apparently doesn't qualify

100
00:11:33.160 --> 00:11:40.940
as a catastrophe because the deaths are distributed, quiet, and polite, and your species only responds to deaths that are

101
00:11:40.940 --> 00:11:48.800
concentrated, loud, and rude. The mechanism is novel, and humans are suspicious of things they haven't tried before (odd,

102
00:11:49.740 --> 00:11:55.040
for a species that invented bungee jumping). And nobody wants to sign first.

103
00:11:55.780 --> 00:12:00.640
Everybody wants to sign second. This is a fine strategy for a conga line.

104
00:12:03.300 --> 00:12:11.640
Why a one percent Treaty Might Beat the Odds. It's self-funding: the health dividends exceed the "cost," so you make money

105
00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:15.780
by doing the right thing, which is the only way your species does the right thing.

106
00:12:16.420 --> 00:12:23.260
"Should we cure more diseases?" polls well across every political spectrum, alongside "Is water wet?"

107
00:12:23.260 --> 00:12:31.360
and "Should we pay Congress less?" It has a referendum pathway that bypasses government resistance (politicians hate this,

108
00:12:31.360 --> 00:12:38.820
which is how you know it works). It has visible beneficiaries: one hundred and fifty thousand deaths per day.

109
00:12:39.340 --> 00:12:46.500
And each signing country makes the next more likely, because peer pressure is humanity's most reliable technology and the

110
00:12:46.500 --> 00:12:51.820
reason your teenagers wear the same shoes. Probability Estimates.

111
00:12:51.860 --> 00:12:59.400
Economists would find the following defensible (and economists find very little defensible, which is their brand): The pessimistic

112
00:12:59.400 --> 00:13:03.120
floor scenario has a probability of zero point one percent.

113
00:13:03.900 --> 00:13:08.940
Translation: Everything goes wrong. Global crises. Political gridlock.

114
00:13:09.200 --> 00:13:14.980
Asteroid, possibly. The conservative central scenario has a probability of one percent.

115
00:13:15.800 --> 00:13:21.840
Translation: Failure remains the base case. Still five hundred and three times better than bed nets.

116
00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:27.140
The optimistic ceiling scenario has a probability of twenty-five percent.

117
00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:32.620
Translation: A major crisis opens a political window. Or humans just...

118
00:13:33.020 --> 00:13:43.640
decide to be smart for once. The model uses: Central estimate one percent Expected Value (Even When You Assume Failure).

119
00:13:44.220 --> 00:13:52.820
At one percent probability of success, expected R.O.I. is eight hundred and forty-eight thousand to one (still positive,

120
00:13:52.980 --> 00:14:01.340
even assuming near-certain failure) and expected cost per disability-adjusted life year, or D.A.L.Y., is zero point one

121
00:14:01.340 --> 00:14:08.960
seven seven dollars, which is five hundred and three times better than bed nets at eighty-nine dollars per D.A.L.Y.

122
00:14:09.740 --> 00:14:14.420
Downside: capped at about one billion dollars. You lose the cost of a nice yacht.

123
00:14:15.160 --> 00:14:23.860
Upside: unbounded. You cure most diseases. Even assuming failure is the base case, this beats the gold standard of global

124
00:14:23.860 --> 00:14:30.130
health interventions. The downside is a yacht. The upside is the end of most disease.

125
00:14:30.760 --> 00:14:38.300
Your species makes worse bets every day in Las Vegas, except there the upside is a slightly larger pile of papers.

126
00:14:38.860 --> 00:14:46.540
The yacht will be fine. It was going to depreciate anyway. The Chain Reaction Model: Why This Idea Cannot Stay Contained.

127
00:14:47.160 --> 00:14:54.760
Your species has a cognitive immune system. When someone suggests redirecting one percent of murder money to medicine money,

128
00:14:55.340 --> 00:14:59.240
approximately ninety percent of humans will dismiss it without engaging.

129
00:15:00.180 --> 00:15:07.940
This is the institutionalization rate: the probability that a human's brain classifies "stop funding murder" as crazier

130
00:15:07.940 --> 00:15:13.040
than "continue funding murder." On Earth, this is called "being realistic."

131
00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:20.200
The question is whether a ninety percent dismissal rate prevents the idea from reaching someone who can act on it.

132
00:15:21.100 --> 00:15:24.960
This section answers whether reaching such people is overwhelmingly likely.

133
00:15:25.540 --> 00:15:31.740
(The next section addresses what happens once they understand the incentive structure.) Direct Encounters.

134
00:15:32.700 --> 00:15:40.480
On your planet, important humans find ideas through news, conferences, advisors whispering in their ears, or occasionally

135
00:15:40.480 --> 00:15:47.120
by accident while searching for something else (this is how most of your scientific breakthroughs happen, and also how you

136
00:15:47.120 --> 00:15:54.420
discovered penicillin). Each of two thousand nine hundred and eighty potential implementers (two thousand seven hundred

137
00:15:54.420 --> 00:16:01.980
and eighty billionaires plus one hundred and ninety-five heads of state) has an annual probability zero point five percent

138
00:16:01.980 --> 00:16:06.960
of stumbling across this idea. That is a very small number per person.

139
00:16:07.220 --> 00:16:15.820
But there are a lot of persons. The Mathematics. The probability that a given implementer encounters the idea within ten

140
00:16:15.820 --> 00:16:24.300
years: The per-implementer probability of engaging (encountering AND not dismissing): The expected number of implementers

141
00:16:24.300 --> 00:16:29.420
who engage: The probability that at least one engages: one hundred percent.

142
00:16:30.400 --> 00:16:37.520
That is not a typo. With two thousand nine hundred and eighty potential implementers and a four point eight nine percent

143
00:16:37.800 --> 00:16:44.980
per-implementer encounter probability over ten years, the model expects approximately fourteen point five implementers to

144
00:16:44.980 --> 00:16:48.520
engage with this idea. Not guaranteed in the mathematical sense.

145
00:16:49.080 --> 00:16:55.820
But in planning terms, this is the kind of number you treat as near-inevitable unless your diffusion assumptions are wrong.

146
00:16:56.560 --> 00:17:00.120
The dismissal rate stops mattering once enough shots are on goal.

147
00:17:01.120 --> 00:17:05.980
This model answers only: "Is it overwhelmingly likely to reach someone who matters?"

148
00:17:06.339 --> 00:17:11.280
The answer is yes. The next section asks: "What happens once it does?"

149
00:17:12.300 --> 00:17:19.260
This model is deliberately conservative in three ways: Base engagement rate: Uses the same engagement rate for personal

150
00:17:19.260 --> 00:17:25.720
recommendations as for passive exposure (personal recommendations convert at approximately three times higher rates).

151
00:17:26.560 --> 00:17:33.220
Narrow implementer definition: Counts only billionaires and heads of state (excludes foundation heads, W.H.O.

152
00:17:33.220 --> 00:17:41.260
officials, major fund managers, et cetera). No compounding: Ignores the feedback loop where media coverage of early adoption

153
00:17:41.260 --> 00:17:47.740
increases subsequent encounter rates, and ignores network amplification (people sharing the idea with others).

154
00:17:49.000 --> 00:17:53.320
The Decision-Theoretic Case: Why Rational Actors Eventually Move.

155
00:17:54.020 --> 00:18:01.480
The chain reaction model says approximately fourteen point five implementers will probably engage with this idea.

156
00:18:01.620 --> 00:18:08.580
The next question is what they do once they understand it. On Wishonia, they would act immediately, because on Wishonia

157
00:18:08.580 --> 00:18:16.580
we are not insane. On Earth, you need to prove that acting is profitable, because your species will not save itself for

158
00:18:16.580 --> 00:18:23.060
free. The Incentive Structure. Most "save the world" pitches are public-good pleas.

159
00:18:24.100 --> 00:18:30.260
Public goods have a free-rider problem: everyone wants the bridge, nobody wants to pay for the bridge, and so the bridge

160
00:18:30.260 --> 00:18:35.080
doesn't get built and everyone drowns in the river while complimenting each other's fiscal prudence.

161
00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:40.920
This design is different. It routes part of the upside back to whoever moves first.

162
00:18:41.740 --> 00:18:48.960
If you act and others also act: the treaty passes, you capture two hundred and seventy-two percent annual returns as an

163
00:18:48.960 --> 00:18:56.880
early backer, plus humanity gets healthier (side benefit). If you act and no one else acts: you still bought a cheap option

164
00:18:56.880 --> 00:19:02.000
on the largest health intervention in history. The yacht money was going to depreciate anyway.

165
00:19:02.680 --> 00:19:08.600
If you wait and others also act: someone else gets the returns, the credit, and the Wikipedia entry.

166
00:19:09.080 --> 00:19:12.040
You get to explain that you were "still doing due diligence."

167
00:19:12.760 --> 00:19:18.290
If you wait and no one else acts: nothing changes. One hundred and fifty thousand deaths a day continue.

168
00:19:19.220 --> 00:19:25.220
You remain rich, mortal, and forgettable. This does not make action a mathematical certainty.

169
00:19:25.700 --> 00:19:32.140
It makes action the obviously attractive move for anyone who can read a spreadsheet and prefers not dying.

170
00:19:32.980 --> 00:19:40.940
Why the expected value, or E.V. Is Not Just Moral. "Good for humanity" has never been enough to make humans act.

171
00:19:41.500 --> 00:19:46.120
If it were, you would have cured malaria with the money you spend on novelty phone cases.

172
00:19:46.660 --> 00:19:53.300
The mechanism has to pay people, because your species requires payment for everything including not dying.

173
00:19:54.260 --> 00:20:02.280
For funders: VICTORY bondholders are modeled at two hundred and seventy-two percent annual returns if the treaty passes.

174
00:20:02.900 --> 00:20:06.440
That is the price of being early to the largest health intervention in history.

175
00:20:07.120 --> 00:20:13.940
For politicians: two point seven two billion dollars a year in campaign support for legislators who voted yes.

176
00:20:14.400 --> 00:20:17.920
Reelection fuel. The only substance politicians metabolize.

177
00:20:18.420 --> 00:20:25.940
For governments: they trade a small fraction of murder budget for lower disease burden, visible domestic wins, and twenty-seven

178
00:20:25.940 --> 00:20:29.850
point two billion dollars a year in new health-research funding.

179
00:20:30.160 --> 00:20:33.600
This is the kind of deal where saying no requires active effort.

180
00:20:34.220 --> 00:20:38.440
The claim is not "humanity benefits, therefore billionaires act."

181
00:20:39.040 --> 00:20:46.060
Your species is not built that way, and pretending otherwise is how you got fifty years of unmet zero point seven percent

182
00:20:46.460 --> 00:20:54.460
official development assistance, or O.D.A. targets. The claim is: this architecture pays funders, politicians, and states

183
00:20:54.460 --> 00:20:58.720
enough that someone with resources has a reason to move first.

184
00:20:59.500 --> 00:21:02.560
Why This Should Matter Even to a Self-Interested Billionaire.

185
00:21:03.280 --> 00:21:07.440
If you are extremely rich, the relevant question is not "Is this charitable?"

186
00:21:08.260 --> 00:21:15.940
Charity is what you do to feel better at galas. The relevant question is: "Does this improve the expected future for me,

187
00:21:16.300 --> 00:21:20.980
my family, and the pile of assets I'm sitting on like a dragon who read an economics textbook?".

188
00:21:22.360 --> 00:21:29.700
Your family is made of biology. Private jets, private islands, and private security do not protect your children from cancer,

189
00:21:30.300 --> 00:21:35.120
neurodegeneration, autoimmune disease, antibiotic resistance, or aging.

190
00:21:35.900 --> 00:21:42.520
Redirecting twenty-seven point two billion dollars per year into treatment discovery is not altruism in the abstract.

191
00:21:43.320 --> 00:21:48.480
It is a direct attack on the probability that everyone you love dies the normal human way.

192
00:21:49.140 --> 00:21:55.880
Your fortune sits on top of the productive economy. When the destructive/parasitic economy reaches equilibrium with the

193
00:21:56.060 --> 00:21:59.580
productive one, wealth preservation gets harder for everyone.

194
00:22:00.220 --> 00:22:07.400
The current destructive economy is already thirteen point two trillion dollars per year, or eleven point five percent of

195
00:22:07.400 --> 00:22:14.140
G.D.P., and the model projects it reaching the failed-state danger zone in eight years if the loop is not broken.

196
00:22:15.100 --> 00:22:22.200
The upside is macro-scale, not boutique-scale. The twenty-year model projects the Treaty Path producing sixteen point five

197
00:22:22.200 --> 00:22:30.300
times the G.D.P. of doing nothing. The fuller Wishonia path, where the political dysfunction tax is also removed, projects

198
00:22:30.300 --> 00:22:38.360
fifty-six point seven times. The broader system is currently bleeding one hundred and one trillion dollars in opportunity

199
00:22:38.360 --> 00:22:46.900
cost, or eighty-seven point eight percent of global G.D.P. If you are rich because you own claims on future productive output,

200
00:22:47.320 --> 00:22:54.740
this matters to you personally. In other words: this is not "give up one percent of military spending so strangers can feel

201
00:22:54.740 --> 00:23:03.540
better." It is "buy a claim on the healthier, richer, less collapse-prone future in which your family survives longer and

202
00:23:03.540 --> 00:23:06.920
your assets sit on top of a civilization that still functions.".

203
00:23:07.900 --> 00:23:13.680
Conditional Near-Inevitability Claim. On Wishonia, we would call this "stating the obvious."

204
00:23:14.480 --> 00:23:21.500
On Earth, you need formal logic to accept that rich people who prefer not dying will eventually invest in not dying.

205
00:23:22.400 --> 00:23:29.760
Fine. Here is the formal logic. Let X be the minimum number of rich, capable humans needed to finance a one billion dollar

206
00:23:29.760 --> 00:23:36.540
campaign. In the current model, X equals four. The pool of plausibly aligned principals is thirty.

207
00:23:37.040 --> 00:23:44.880
You need four out of thirty. Your species has worse odds at every casino on the Las Vegas Strip and you still show up.

208
00:23:45.560 --> 00:23:52.920
If: Rich humans prefer living. They recognize that redirecting twenty-seven point two billion dollars per year into treatment

209
00:23:52.920 --> 00:24:01.000
discovery improves the odds for everyone they love. (If this premise is wrong, your species has larger problems than this

210
00:24:01.000 --> 00:24:09.000
treaty.). The macro upside is directionally correct. Treaty Path: sixteen point five times the G.D.P.

211
00:24:09.220 --> 00:24:14.580
of doing nothing after twenty years. Wishonia path: fifty-six point seven times.

212
00:24:15.860 --> 00:24:24.740
The collapse-risk model is directionally correct. The destructive/parasitic economy is already thirteen point two trillion

213
00:24:24.740 --> 00:24:33.280
dollars per year (eleven point five percent of G.D.P.) and trends toward the failed-state danger zone within eight years.

214
00:24:34.060 --> 00:24:43.180
Enough upside is privately capturable. Funders get financial returns, reputational credit, agenda-setting power, and the

215
00:24:43.180 --> 00:24:48.620
continued existence of the productive economy their fortunes sit on top of.

216
00:24:49.140 --> 00:24:58.160
At least X rational actors are persuaded. Four billionaires or heads of state understand premises one through four.

217
00:24:59.180 --> 00:25:07.220
Execution friction stays below surplus. Coordination costs and political friction don't eat the entire upside.

218
00:25:07.880 --> 00:25:12.260
(They never have for any project with this ratio of cost to return.).

219
00:25:13.000 --> 00:25:17.040
Then the campaign launches. Not because every billionaire acts.

220
00:25:17.560 --> 00:25:26.520
Because only four need to, the downside is capped at one billion dollars (one yacht), and the upside is measured in survival.

221
00:25:27.820 --> 00:25:37.180
If the number of persuaded actors materially exceeds four, then failure requires a surprisingly specific story: simultaneous

222
00:25:37.180 --> 00:25:45.780
breakdown in persuasion, coordination, incentive capture, and execution across the entire aligned pool.

223
00:25:46.440 --> 00:25:50.930
On Wishonia, we have a word for betting on that kind of simultaneous failure.

224
00:25:51.580 --> 00:25:57.600
The word translates roughly to "stupid.". Minimal Launch Probability Model.

225
00:25:58.500 --> 00:26:03.020
Forget the planet. Forget the average billionaire (he's busy buying a sports team).

226
00:26:03.920 --> 00:26:11.300
Ask only: does a launch coalition form from the tiny slice of rich humans who already care about not dying?

227
00:26:12.020 --> 00:26:17.740
In the current minimal model: Plausible high-alignment principal pool: thirty people.

228
00:26:18.800 --> 00:26:26.280
Required lead principals: four. Expected ready launch principals within ten years: five point eight five.

229
00:26:27.100 --> 00:26:31.460
Probability a launch coalition forms: eighty-six point three percent.

230
00:26:32.160 --> 00:26:37.920
In plain English: you need four people out of thirty who already agree with the premise.

231
00:26:38.440 --> 00:26:45.540
On current assumptions, that coalition forms with probability eighty-six point three percent within ten years.

232
00:26:46.080 --> 00:26:52.820
This is like asking whether at least three people in a room of health-obsessed billionaires will invest in health.

233
00:26:53.640 --> 00:27:00.860
Your species asks harder questions at trivia night. This is not treaty ratification probability.

234
00:27:00.980 --> 00:27:06.920
It is the probability that enough capable humans emerge to fund a serious campaign.

235
00:27:07.580 --> 00:27:15.940
If this number is high, the objection changes from "nobody will touch this" to "who moves first, and how long do they pretend

236
00:27:15.940 --> 00:27:25.540
to think about it?". Why Delay Becomes Irrational. Once you believe a coalition is likely to form anyway, waiting stops

237
00:27:25.540 --> 00:27:32.670
looking prudent and starts looking like the time you didn't buy Apple stock because you wanted to "see how it plays out.".

238
00:27:33.140 --> 00:27:40.260
Move early: You define the structure, recruit the coalition, capture the largest returns, and get the Wikipedia entry that

239
00:27:40.260 --> 00:27:46.840
says "one of the people who ended most disease." Your grandchildren reference it at dinner parties forever.

240
00:27:47.300 --> 00:27:53.480
Wait: Someone else writes the terms, gets the credit, and locks in first-mover influence.

241
00:27:54.200 --> 00:28:00.360
You explain to your grandchildren that you were "monitoring the situation," and they nod politely while looking at their

242
00:28:00.360 --> 00:28:08.260
phones. The question is no longer "Will anyone fund this?" It is "Do I want to be one of the four who shaped the largest

243
00:28:08.260 --> 00:28:14.280
health intervention in history, or the person who arrived after the structure was set and asked if there were still tickets?".

244
00:28:16.140 --> 00:28:25.000
What Would Have To Be Wrong. If no serious actor moves within ten years, then at least one of the following is true: One.

245
00:28:25.000 --> 00:28:30.380
The expected-value math is wrong. The R.O.I., health gains, or funding leverage do not hold.

246
00:28:30.920 --> 00:28:36.270
Check the math in the appendix. Two. The idea never reached enough decision-makers.

247
00:28:36.860 --> 00:28:43.380
The diffusion model failed in the real world. Three. The private-incentive bridge is weaker than modeled.

248
00:28:44.040 --> 00:28:48.420
The surplus exists, but not enough of it is capturable by first movers.

249
00:28:48.880 --> 00:28:58.100
Four. Hidden costs dominate the visible upside. Reputational risk, retaliation, legal friction, or coordination costs are

250
00:28:58.100 --> 00:29:04.840
larger than assumed. Five. Enough elite actors prefer inertia to asymmetrical upside.

251
00:29:05.400 --> 00:29:12.980
Possible. But across a plausible aligned tail of thirty principals inside a broader universe of two thousand nine hundred

252
00:29:12.980 --> 00:29:19.160
and eighty implementers over ten years, it is not the first explanation a rational model should reach for.

253
00:29:20.280 --> 00:29:27.940
Your task, if you want to reject this, is to pick one. Not to gesture vaguely at "complexity," which is what humans say

254
00:29:27.940 --> 00:29:33.860
when they mean "I don't want to think about it." Pick the failure mode.

255
00:29:33.860 --> 00:29:35.500
Show your work. Your species demands this of schoolchildren.

256
00:29:36.360 --> 00:29:39.450
Surely you can manage it for a question about whether to cure diseases.

257
00:29:41.200 --> 00:29:45.620
Why This Still Feels Inevitable. This is still a probabilistic story.

258
00:29:46.500 --> 00:29:53.300
But once diffusion and incentives are combined, it stops looking like a fragile moonshot and starts looking like a machine

259
00:29:53.300 --> 00:29:57.540
that is hard to keep dormant. The chain reaction model handles reach.

260
00:29:58.140 --> 00:30:03.440
The incentive design handles motion. The question stops being "can this happen?"

261
00:30:03.640 --> 00:30:11.060
and starts being "how long can everyone with money, ambition, and pattern recognition keep pretending this is not in front

262
00:30:11.060 --> 00:30:17.870
of them?". Your species has a peculiar habit of treating path dependence as optional.

263
00:30:17.940 --> 00:30:23.240
It isn't. Once a mechanism pays people to expand it, the default trajectory changes.

264
00:30:23.800 --> 00:30:28.700
This is how you built railroads, credit markets, social networks, and defense industries.

265
00:30:29.180 --> 00:30:33.640
A system that pays people to fund health instead of weapons is not a proof.

266
00:30:34.220 --> 00:30:40.100
It is something more dangerous: a machine with incentives pointing in the right direction.

267
00:30:41.200 --> 00:30:47.340
Why The Treaty Won't Stop at one percent Most international commitments stagnate after signing.

268
00:30:48.000 --> 00:30:51.660
You saw the depressing table. zero point seven percent O.D.A.

269
00:30:51.920 --> 00:30:56.480
targets unmet for fifty-plus years. Kyoto abandoned. Paris missed.

270
00:30:57.160 --> 00:31:01.340
Your track record with follow-through makes goldfish look disciplined.

271
00:31:01.340 --> 00:31:07.950
So why would a one percent health treaty be any different? Because this time, people get paid.

272
00:31:08.220 --> 00:31:15.780
The eighty-ten-ten funding structure allocates ten percent of treaty funding (two point seven two billion dollars per year

273
00:31:15.780 --> 00:31:23.980
at one percent) to Incentive Alignment Bonds. These are securities that pay out when treaties *expand*, not just maintain.

274
00:31:24.640 --> 00:31:32.340
People get rich when more diseases get cured. It only took your species four hundred years of financial instruments to produce

275
00:31:32.340 --> 00:31:38.240
one that doesn't require someone to suffer (progress). The Expansion Mechanism.

276
00:31:38.300 --> 00:31:46.500
At a one percent treaty level, the annual I.A.B. pool is two point seven two billion dollars per year, creating strong political

277
00:31:46.500 --> 00:31:53.000
pressure, ten times the largest N.G.O. budgets. At a two percent level, the annual I.A.B.

278
00:31:53.280 --> 00:32:00.640
pool is five point four four billion dollars per year, creating very strong political pressure at a nation-state level.

279
00:32:01.160 --> 00:32:09.520
At a five percent level, the annual I.A.B. pool is thirteen point six billion dollars per year, creating overwhelming political

280
00:32:09.520 --> 00:32:16.020
pressure equivalent to a major industry lobby. At a ten percent level, the annual I.A.B.

281
00:32:16.300 --> 00:32:22.910
pool is twenty-seven point two billion dollars per year, which is probably irresistible and has no precedent.

282
00:32:23.680 --> 00:32:29.640
Why This Differs from Every Other Treaty. zero point seven percent O.D.A.

283
00:32:29.640 --> 00:32:36.260
failed because nobody made money when countries complied. The N.G.O.s pushing for compliance run on guilt.

284
00:32:36.580 --> 00:32:40.950
Guilt has terrible fuel efficiency. Guilt gets you to the gym in January.

285
00:32:41.680 --> 00:32:46.330
Greed gets you there in July. Guilt volunteers at the soup kitchen once.

286
00:32:47.020 --> 00:32:52.400
Greed builds the soup kitchen and names it after itself. This treaty runs on greed.

287
00:32:53.640 --> 00:32:56.920
The one percent health treaty creates its own expansion lobby.

288
00:32:57.540 --> 00:33:03.080
Bond holders profit when the treaty expands to two percent, then five percent, then ten percent.

289
00:33:03.700 --> 00:33:11.000
These aren't activists you can ignore at a dinner party. They're investors with quarterly earnings calls about curing diseases.

290
00:33:11.440 --> 00:33:17.960
They have Bloomberg terminals. They have lobbyists. They have the same tools that weapons manufacturers currently use to

291
00:33:17.960 --> 00:33:22.920
expand military budgets, except pointed in the opposite direction.

292
00:33:23.300 --> 00:33:30.100
The Ratchet Effect. Once one percent passes, two point seven two billion dollars per year flows to I.A.B.

293
00:33:30.120 --> 00:33:36.860
holders pushing for two percent. When two percent passes, five point four four billion dollars per year pushes for five

294
00:33:36.860 --> 00:33:43.040
percent. The engine runs on compound greed, which is like compound interest but for not dying.

295
00:33:43.820 --> 00:33:50.760
The politicians feel it too. The treaty's ten percent political incentive allocation scales identically: at one percent,

296
00:33:51.000 --> 00:33:56.320
it's two point seven two billion dollars per year in campaign support for legislators who voted yes.

297
00:33:56.760 --> 00:34:04.760
At five percent, five times that. A politician who votes to expand the treaty is voting to expand their own reelection budget.

298
00:34:05.340 --> 00:34:09.800
You didn't change what politicians are. You changed what they're rewarded for.

299
00:34:10.620 --> 00:34:15.550
Most treaties are gym memberships purchased in January and abandoned by February.

300
00:34:15.699 --> 00:34:19.400
This one has a constituency that *grows* with each success.

301
00:34:20.040 --> 00:34:26.500
On Wishonia, we find this arrangement embarrassing. On Earth, it's the only arrangement that works.

302
00:34:27.000 --> 00:34:35.060
Long-Term Feasibility. With I.A.B.s, the ten percent allocation creates a perpetual political engine.

303
00:34:35.440 --> 00:34:40.120
A machine that runs on greed and outputs health. Embarrassing, but effective.

304
00:34:41.080 --> 00:34:45.760
The most effective things your species has ever built have all been embarrassing.

305
00:34:46.400 --> 00:34:50.980
The internet was built for sharing research papers and is used for arguing with strangers.

306
00:34:51.860 --> 00:34:56.400
Democracy was built for collective wisdom and is used for electing celebrities.

307
00:34:57.280 --> 00:35:01.720
This machine is built for curing disease and is powered by selfishness.

308
00:35:02.180 --> 00:35:10.410
The pattern holds. Without I.A.B.s, this joins Kyoto in the Museum of Things Humanity Promised to Do and Then Didn't.

309
00:35:11.120 --> 00:35:16.080
The museum is very large. It has a gift shop. The gift shop sells promises.
